WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
America’s schools spend lots of money,

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:05.340
but often, barely educate kids.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.800
Our public schools are failing

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.500
Philadelphia’s failing public school district

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.980
I went to the neighborhood school

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.500
Elaine Wells is a mom in Philadelphia.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.900
There were fights after school every day.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.460
It was horrible.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.700
So she walked her son to another school.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.020
We went to go enroll and we were told,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.740
"No, he can't go here.” That was my wake up call.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.920
She’d discovered that although with most services,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.280
we get to shop around,

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.660
that's not true for so-called “public schools.”

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.000
Instead, parents are often stuck with the

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.290
school the government assigns them.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.620
But a new kind of school has been changing that...

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.629
charter schools.

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:47.280
It's funded with taxpayer money,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.760
but Hardy gets to run it largely like a private school.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:52.600
In Philadelphia,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.920
David Hardy created a charter he calls Boy’s Latin.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.930
It tries new ideas.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.210
People in government run schools don't like to change

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:03.700
They also don’t like competition from charters.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.400
But charters are often better and parents line up,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.760
hoping to get their kids admitted.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.300
A lottery will decide which students will stay or go.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.180
This lottery was in New York.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.660
Congratulations.

00:01:16.662 --> 00:01:18.520
The winners are ecstatic,

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.920
but there are many more losers.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:21.980
You're on pins and needles

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.700
and you're hoping and you're praying

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.260
A charter school lottery left thousands

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.920
of Philadelphia students disappointed last night.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.540
It's like, you don't have a chance.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.760
Elaine’s kids lost many lotteries.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.300
It's heartbreaking.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.640
[Music]

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.509
But finally, they were admitted to David Hardy’s school

00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:39.460
Shirts are tucked in.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.520
Yep.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:41.300
That's important to you.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.060
Yeah.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.120
And the kids, they don't mind it?

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.740
Of course, they mind it. They're teenage boys.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:49.820
The rules are there to kind of set the stage

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:50.800
for the students.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.440
If the teacher can tell you to tuck in their shirt,

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.600
they can tell you to be quiet in class.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.360
They can tell you to do your homework.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.560
It gives us some authority over the kids.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.100
Elaine was so eager for her sons to attend,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.260
she arranged to have Ibrahim repeat the 6th grade.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:08.750
I bet you didn't like that.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.780
No, not at all.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.900
That's when I really...

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:12.980
that was the moment where I

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.480
most despised Boys' Latin in every possible way

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.600
But their attitudes quickly changed

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.420
Before Boys Latin I would come home and say,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:21.820
"Okay, I need you to read for an hour,

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:22.460
read a book."

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.940
And their response would be "Why? What did we do?"

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.100
Like reading was a punishment.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.940
Right. So getting into Boys Latin,

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.540
I would find books in the bathroom on the floor.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.440
Came to the point where the teacher would tell our mom

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:36.720
that I'd taken too many books.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.840
And then there were strange nighttime phone calls.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.209
He's in his room and I hear him talking on

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:44.600
the phone and it was like 10:00 o'clock

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:48.000
at night and I'm like "Who are you on the phone with?"

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.640
And he was like,

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.720
"Well, Mr. Bumbulsky told me to call him if I

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.720
needed help with homework."

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.180
You thought he was bothering the teacher?

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.900
Yes. Yes.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.320
Another change was the kids

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.080
spending more time in school.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.960
They’re here from 8 am to often 5 pm.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.080
And they have to take Latin.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.190
It's ridiculous. Nobody speaks Latin.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.840
Well we picked Latin because it was hard.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.480
What's the point of that?

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:12.880
Because life is hard --

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.280
because in order to be prepared you have to work hard.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:17.840
And we wanted to get that

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.060
into the psyche of our students

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.380
Do you feel sorry for your friends

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.240
who are still stuck in the old...

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.160
Yeah, it's kind of sad because

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.240
I'm looking at them like,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.520
"What do you mean you're taking algebra one

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.909
in senior year? You're supposed to be taking pre-calc”

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:34.160
We deliver. Since the very first class

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.520
we've sent more black boys to college

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.069
than any high school in Pennsylvania.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:41.240
And yet, now Philadelphia,

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:42.400
and many other cities

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:45.269
are rejecting new charter applications.

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:47.360
Philadelphia rejected David Hardy’s plan

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.110
to open a Girls' Latin.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.510
You’re succeeding and they’re trying to kill you off

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.920
Because they realize that if we continue to

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.400
take children away, they won't have jobs.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.120
Most studies on charters find increases in

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.820
test scores. Kids don’t learn as well

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.629
in most government run schools

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:07.220
There's always a reason

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.820
in a government-run school for not serving kids.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.380
They'll tell you what's wrong with the kid.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:16.090
They never say “it's my fault.”

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.090
Instead –

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:19.229
Students are going to&nbsp;march for&nbsp;more&nbsp;funding

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:21.709
The government schools say they need more money

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:23.760
Members&nbsp;of&nbsp;the Philadelphia&nbsp;teachers&nbsp;union

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.220
push&nbsp;for&nbsp;more&nbsp;funding

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.440
More funds? Get this:

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.540
Philly schools already spend almost $19,000 per child.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.880
Do the math, that’s about half a million dollars

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:39.290
per classroom. Where does the money go?

00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.260
They have a director of special ed

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:44.591
and they have a assistant director a special ed.

00:04:44.591 --> 00:04:47.260
They may have a director of high school athletics

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.540
and they have an assistant.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.389
And they have all these -- a lot of overhead.

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:52.580
To preserve their jobs,

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.590
government teachers now say this:

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.030
the publicly funded privately managed charter schools

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.340
drain scarce resources from other public schools.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.220
Drain public funds.

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:05.000
You can’t tell me that charter schools

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.920
are taking funding from public schools.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.580
Every parent pays taxes.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.620
It’s the taxes that we pay that fund the school system.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.180
So if my child, If I choose for my child

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.700
to go to a charter school,

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.080
then that's where my taxes should go.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.120
That makes sense,

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.240
but Philadelphia and other cities

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.140
don’t even give charters the same amount of money

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:27.960
they give to schools they control.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.440
They give them less.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.060
In Philadelphia, only 70% of that.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.680
So, the government schools make money

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.169
whenever a kid leaves for a charter.

00:05:38.169 --> 00:05:39.600
Over 13 years of schooling,

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.229
Philadelphia saves&nbsp;$70,000&nbsp;per child.

00:05:44.229 --> 00:05:45.500
What if they gave you the difference?

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:48.120
Yes, absolutely give them the rest of the money

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.680
But it would also mean

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.880
that there would be a whole lot less union jobs.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:56.550
And the unions are not going to be for that.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.030
There are bad charters.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:05:59.500
But bad charters close.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.830
Bad public schools don't close.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.000
That’s a big difference.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.180
When charters don’t deliver for kids,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.830
they go out of business.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:11.180
The government monopoly never goes out of business.

